// What a one-file module refuses, and where each refusal is reported.
//
// Parse stage, which is why it is its own scenario: a parse diagnostic stops the
// compile before name resolution, so nothing here resolves.
//
// The folder holds:
//   misnamed.cone      'mod other': a one-file module is named for its file
//   twice.cone         a one-file module declaring itself a second time
//   clash.cone         'mod clash' beside clash/clash.cone, a module folder of
//                      the same name
//   clash/clash.cone   the module folder, which is the one kept
//   org/nested.cone    'mod nested' in an organisational folder, where no module
//                      may sit
//
// The two diagnostics with no line of their own are in cases.toml: which files a
// module holds is a fact about the filesystem, not a position in a source. Each
// file they name is left out of the compile, since the one thing it says is that
// it is not this module's file.

// A one-file submodule is already a module of this one, bound under the name its
// file gives it. Importing it asks for a second binding of a name this module has
import twice;        //~ ErrorModFile "submodule of this one"

fn designated() i64 {
  1i64;
}
